{% comment %}
  V7 Care Ritual — Dual Track (Thread S4 / B2 / B9).
  Two parallel material ceremonies:
    Track A — Forge Hybrid carbon steel + Kuro Strip blades (metal)
    Track B — Terre Clay vessels (fired earth)
  Tabs switch between tracks. Each track has its own numbered rite sequence.
  Placed below the Kansa companion section on the homepage.
  Motion: inView stagger, Strike audio on tab switches.
  Fully brand-token CSS — no hardcoded hex that conflicts with STYLEGUIDE.
{% endcomment %}

<section
  class="maison-ritual-v7 section section--page-width"
  data-section-id="{{ section.id }}"
  id="care-ritual"
  aria-label="Care Ritual — Material Ceremonies"
>

  {%- comment -%} ── Header ──────────────────────────────────────────── {%- endcomment -%}
  <header class="maison-ritual-v7__header">
    <div class="maison-ritual-v7__kicker-row">
      <span class="maison-ritual-v7__badge">LIFETIME DISCIPLINE</span>
      <span class="maison-ritual-v7__kicker">KANSA CARE PROTOCOLS · MATERIAL CEREMONIES</span>
    </div>
    <h2 class="maison-ritual-v7__title">
      {{ section.settings.heading | default: 'Covenant of the Kitchen' }}
    </h2>
    <p class="maison-ritual-v7__lede">
      {{ section.settings.lede | default: 'Two materials. Two disciplines. One covenant that outlasts a generation of cooking. Choose your surface and follow the rites that keep it alive.' }}
    </p>
  </header>

  {%- comment -%} ── Material Tab Switcher ────────────────────────────── {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="maison-ritual-v7__tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Material track">
    <button
      class="maison-ritual-v7__tab is-active"
      role="tab"
      aria-selected="true"
      aria-controls="ritual-track-metal"
      id="ritual-tab-metal"
      data-track="metal"
      data-strike
    >
      <span class="tab-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-hammer" aria-hidden="true"></i></span>
      <span class="tab-label">Metal</span>
      <span class="tab-sub">Carbon Steel · Bell Bronze · Damascus Blade</span>
    </button>
    <button
      class="maison-ritual-v7__tab"
      role="tab"
      aria-selected="false"
      aria-controls="ritual-track-clay"
      id="ritual-tab-clay"
      data-track="clay"
      data-strike
    >
      <span class="tab-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-dot" aria-hidden="true"></i></span>
      <span class="tab-label">Clay</span>
      <span class="tab-sub">Terre Fired Stoneware · Refractory Vessel</span>
    </button>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%} ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      TRACK A — METAL (Forge Hybrid + Kuro Strip)
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ {%- endcomment -%}
  <div
    class="maison-ritual-v7__track is-active"
    id="ritual-track-metal"
    role="tabpanel"
    aria-labelledby="ritual-tab-metal"
  >

    <div class="maison-ritual-v7__track-intro">
      <div class="track-intro__lineups">
        <span class="lineup-chip lineup-chip--forge">FORGE HYBRID</span>
        <span class="lineup-chip lineup-chip--kuro">KURO STRIP</span>
      </div>
      <p class="track-intro__meta">
        <span class="mono">1.0085 Carbon Steel · VG10 Damascus 60 HRC</span><br>
        No synthetic coatings. No PTFE. No forgiveness. The metal remembers every cook.
      </p>
      <div class="maison-ritual-v7__intro-media">
        <figure class="maison-ritual-v7__intro-figure">
          <img
            src="{{ 'maison-kadai-closeup.webp' | asset_url }}"
            alt="Living Patina &amp; Metallurgy"
            width="600"
            height="400"
            loading="lazy"
            class="maison-ritual-v7__intro-img"
          >
          <figcaption class="maison-ritual-v7__intro-cap">Living acoustic patina &amp; 78:22 bell bronze rim</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="maison-ritual-v7__intro-figure">
          <img
            src="{{ 'maison-handle-engineering.webp' | asset_url }}"
            alt="Handle Thermal Dissipation"
            width="600"
            height="400"
            loading="lazy"
            class="maison-ritual-v7__intro-img"
          >
          <figcaption class="maison-ritual-v7__intro-cap">Stay-cool handle thermal barrier under continuous flame</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="maison-ritual-v7__rites" data-ritual-track="metal">

      {%- comment -%} ── RITE 1: SEASON ── {%- endcomment -%}
      <article class="ritual-rite" data-rite="1">
        <div class="ritual-rite__num-col">
          <span class="ritual-rite__numeral">01</span>
          <div class="ritual-rite__connector" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ritual-rite__body">
          <div class="ritual-rite__eyebrow">
            <span class="ritual-rite__devanagari">संस्कार</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__dot" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__rite-name-en">Season</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="ritual-rite__title">The Polymer Covenant</h3>
          <p class="ritual-rite__desc">
            Carbon steel's pores are open. Struck at 1,100 °C, the mill scale receives a first baptism
            of beeswax or grapeseed oil — heat polymerizes the lipid into a glass-hard molecular chain
            bonded to the iron grain. This is not a coating. It is a transformation. Dark spots are progress;
            even black is correct.
          </p>
          <div class="ritual-rite__specs">
            <dl class="spec-dl">
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>OIL</dt>
                <dd>Grapeseed · Beeswax blend · Flaxseed</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>TEMP</dt>
                <dd>Until wisps of smoke appear (~230 °C)</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>CYCLES</dt>
                <dd>Minimum 2× before first use · 15× for full glass patina</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </div>
          <div class="ritual-rite__tags">
            <span class="ritual-tag">HIGH-HEAT POLYMERIZATION</span>
            <span class="ritual-tag">LIPID GLASS PATINA</span>
          </div>
          <div class="ritual-rite__kuro-note" aria-label="Kuro Strip note">
            <span class="kuro-pill">KURO STRIP</span>
            Blades do not season. Apply camellia mineral oil to the flat of the blade after every wash —
            never heat the knife.
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      {%- comment -%} ── RITE 2: PRE-HEAT ── {%- endcomment -%}
      <article class="ritual-rite" data-rite="2">
        <div class="ritual-rite__num-col">
          <span class="ritual-rite__numeral">02</span>
          <div class="ritual-rite__connector" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ritual-rite__body">
          <div class="ritual-rite__eyebrow">
            <span class="ritual-rite__devanagari">तप</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__dot" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__rite-name-en">Pre-heat</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="ritual-rite__title">Thermal Discipline — The Two-Minute Rule</h3>
          <p class="ritual-rite__desc">
            Dual-mass metallurgy requires two minutes of gradual dry heat before oil enters the vessel.
            Start on medium-low, raise to medium. Oil added too early to a cold pan polymerizes unevenly
            and creates sticky patches. Oil added to a correctly pre-heated pan skates across the surface —
            that is the signal to cook.
          </p>
          <div class="ritual-rite__specs">
            <dl class="spec-dl">
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>DURATION</dt>
                <dd>2 min dry · raise to medium before oil</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>SIGNAL</dt>
                <dd>Water droplets bead and dance (Leidenfrost)</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>CAUTION</dt>
                <dd>No rapid full-blast on induction — thermal shock warps the base</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </div>
          <div class="ritual-rite__tags">
            <span class="ritual-tag">2-MIN GRADUAL PRE-HEAT</span>
            <span class="ritual-tag">LEIDENFROST TEST</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      {%- comment -%} ── RITE 3: COOK ── {%- endcomment -%}
      <article class="ritual-rite" data-rite="3">
        <div class="ritual-rite__num-col">
          <span class="ritual-rite__numeral">03</span>
          <div class="ritual-rite__connector" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ritual-rite__body">
          <div class="ritual-rite__eyebrow">
            <span class="ritual-rite__devanagari">कर्म</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__dot" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__rite-name-en">Cook</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="ritual-rite__title">Acids, Fat, and the First 15 Cycles</h3>
          <p class="ritual-rite__desc">
            Acids strip a young patina. Avoid heavy acidic reductions — wine, vinegar, citrus — during
            the first 15 cooking cycles. After the patina darkens to black glass, the iron is fully
            polymerized and tolerates brief acid contact. Fat amplifies patina: cook with butter and high-smoke
            oils, not water-based sauces, in early service.
          </p>
          <div class="ritual-rite__specs">
            <dl class="spec-dl">
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>AVOID</dt>
                <dd>Wine · Vinegar · Lemon (first 15 cycles)</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>PREFER</dt>
                <dd>Butter · Lard · Ghee · Grapeseed</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>SIGNAL</dt>
                <dd>Surface darkens to even matte black = healthy seasoning</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </div>
          <div class="ritual-rite__tags">
            <span class="ritual-tag">ACID EMBARGO · 15 CYCLES</span>
            <span class="ritual-tag">FAT AMPLIFIES PATINA</span>
          </div>
          <div class="ritual-rite__kuro-note" aria-label="Kuro Strip note">
            <span class="kuro-pill">KURO STRIP</span>
            VG10 Damascus tolerates acidic food briefly. Wipe the flat of the blade immediately after
            cutting citrus, tomatoes, or shallots — acid relief etching is cosmetic, not structural.
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      {%- comment -%} ── RITE 4: CLEAN ── {%- endcomment -%}
      <article class="ritual-rite" data-rite="4">
        <div class="ritual-rite__num-col">
          <span class="ritual-rite__numeral">04</span>
          <div class="ritual-rite__connector" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ritual-rite__body">
          <div class="ritual-rite__eyebrow">
            <span class="ritual-rite__devanagari">शुद्धि</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__dot" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__rite-name-en">Clean</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="ritual-rite__title">Zero Soap. Never Soak.</h3>
          <p class="ritual-rite__desc">
            While the pan is still warm (not raging hot) — deglaze with hot water. The residue lifts cleanly.
            Scrub with a natural bristle pan brush or coarse kosher salt on a folded cotton cloth.
            For stubborn fond, boil a small amount of water in the pan to lift it.
            Soap destroys the lipid patina. The dishwasher kills the steel.
          </p>
          <div class="ritual-rite__specs">
            <dl class="spec-dl">
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>TOOLS</dt>
                <dd>Natural bristle brush · Kosher salt · Hot water</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>FORBIDDEN</dt>
                <dd>Dish soap · Steel wool · Dishwasher · Long soaking</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>RUST?</dt>
                <dd>Salt + oil paste scrub → rinse → stovetop dry → re-season</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </div>
          <div class="ritual-rite__tags">
            <span class="ritual-tag">ZERO SOAP</span>
            <span class="ritual-tag">NEVER SOAK</span>
            <span class="ritual-tag">BRISTLE & SALT ONLY</span>
          </div>
          <div class="ritual-rite__kuro-note" aria-label="Kuro Strip note">
            <span class="kuro-pill">KURO STRIP</span>
            Hand wash the knife with mild soap immediately after cutting — acid proteins on the blade
            accelerate micro-pitting. Never put in the dishwasher. Dry with a lint-free cloth.
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      {%- comment -%} ── RITE 5: DRY ── {%- endcomment -%}
      <article class="ritual-rite" data-rite="5">
        <div class="ritual-rite__num-col">
          <span class="ritual-rite__numeral">05</span>
          <div class="ritual-rite__connector" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ritual-rite__body">
          <div class="ritual-rite__eyebrow">
            <span class="ritual-rite__devanagari">अग्नि</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__dot" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__rite-name-en">Dry</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="ritual-rite__title">Flame Evaporation — 60 Seconds</h3>
          <p class="ritual-rite__desc">
            Towel drying leaves water molecules trapped in the iron grain boundaries invisible to the eye.
            Those molecules nucleate rust within hours. Place the cleaned pan over low heat for exactly 60 seconds.
            Watch the surface go matte. That is the signal — all moisture has vaporized. The pan is safe
            to oil and store.
          </p>
          <div class="ritual-rite__specs">
            <dl class="spec-dl">
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>HEAT</dt>
                <dd>Low flame or induction level 2 · 60 seconds</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>SIGNAL</dt>
                <dd>Surface transitions from wet sheen → uniform matte</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </div>
          <div class="ritual-rite__tags">
            <span class="ritual-tag">60S STOVETOP DRY</span>
            <span class="ritual-tag">RUST PREVENTION</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      {%- comment -%} ── RITE 6: OIL & STORE ── {%- endcomment -%}
      <article class="ritual-rite" data-rite="6">
        <div class="ritual-rite__num-col">
          <span class="ritual-rite__numeral">06</span>
          <!-- no connector on last rite -->
        </div>
        <div class="ritual-rite__body">
          <div class="ritual-rite__eyebrow">
            <span class="ritual-rite__devanagari">स्नेह</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__dot" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__rite-name-en">Oil & Store</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="ritual-rite__title">The Resting Membrane</h3>
          <p class="ritual-rite__desc">
            While still warm: drop a single half-teaspoon of high-smoke oil onto the cooking face.
            Buff it thoroughly with a folded cotton cloth — keep buffing until the surface looks bone dry.
            If it looks greasy, you used too much; that oil will go rancid. Store in open air, never
            sealed in a drawer without ventilation. Ready for the next call at the pass.
          </p>
          <div class="ritual-rite__specs">
            <dl class="spec-dl">
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>OIL</dt>
                <dd>Half-teaspoon · Grapeseed, beeswax, or camellia mineral</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>TEST</dt>
                <dd>Surface must look bone dry after buffing — not greasy</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>STORE</dt>
                <dd>Open air · Avoid sealed drawers · Hang or rack preferred</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </div>
          <div class="ritual-rite__tags">
            <span class="ritual-tag">RESTING LIPID MEMBRANE</span>
            <span class="ritual-tag">BONE-DRY RULE</span>
          </div>
          <div class="ritual-rite__kuro-note" aria-label="Kuro Strip note">
            <span class="kuro-pill">KURO STRIP</span>
            Hone at 15° per side on a 1 000/6 000 ceramic whetstone when the blade drags on paper.
            Apply a thin film of camellia mineral oil to the flat before storage. Never cut frozen food or hard bones.
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

    </div>{%- comment -%} /rites metal {%- endcomment -%}
  </div>{%- comment -%} /track metal {%- endcomment -%}


  {%- comment -%} ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      TRACK B — CLAY (Terre Clay)
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ {%- endcomment -%}
  <div
    class="maison-ritual-v7__track"
    id="ritual-track-clay"
    role="tabpanel"
    aria-labelledby="ritual-tab-clay"
    hidden
  >

    <div class="maison-ritual-v7__track-intro">
      <div class="track-intro__lineups">
        <span class="lineup-chip lineup-chip--terre">TERRE CLAY</span>
      </div>
      <p class="track-intro__meta">
        <span class="mono">High-fire stoneware · Feldspathic glaze · Lead-free vitrified slip</span><br>
        Clay remembers heat differently. Never shock it. Never rush it. The vessel is a living object — thermal patience is not optional.
      </p>
      <div class="maison-ritual-v7__intro-media">
        <figure class="maison-ritual-v7__intro-figure">
          <img
            src="{{ 'maison-clay-openpot.webp' | asset_url }}"
            alt="Terre Clay Refractory Vessel"
            width="600"
            height="400"
            loading="lazy"
            class="maison-ritual-v7__intro-img"
          >
          <figcaption class="maison-ritual-v7__intro-cap">High-fire refractory stoneware shell for slow-heat simmering</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="maison-ritual-v7__intro-figure">
          <img
            src="{{ 'maison-clay-potter-craft.webp' | asset_url }}"
            alt="Handcrafted Pottery Wheel Ceremony"
            width="600"
            height="400"
            loading="lazy"
            class="maison-ritual-v7__intro-img"
          >
          <figcaption class="maison-ritual-v7__intro-cap">Master potter shaping raw earthenware on traditional wheel</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="maison-ritual-v7__rites" data-ritual-track="clay">

      {%- comment -%} ── CLAY RITE 1: THE FIRST SOAK ── {%- endcomment -%}
      <article class="ritual-rite ritual-rite--clay" data-rite="1">
        <div class="ritual-rite__num-col">
          <span class="ritual-rite__numeral">01</span>
          <div class="ritual-rite__connector" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ritual-rite__body">
          <div class="ritual-rite__eyebrow">
            <span class="ritual-rite__devanagari">जल संस्कार</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__dot" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__rite-name-en">First Soak</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="ritual-rite__title">Seasoning the Earth — Water Cure</h3>
          <p class="ritual-rite__desc">
            Before first use: fully submerge the vessel in cold water for 15 minutes. The unglazed exterior
            and base absorb water into the fired clay matrix. This creates a thermal buffer — the absorbed moisture
            vaporizes slowly during cooking, distributing heat evenly and preventing micro-fracture from localised
            thermal stress. This is the clay's equivalent of seasoning.
          </p>
          <div class="ritual-rite__specs">
            <dl class="spec-dl">
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>WATER</dt>
                <dd>Cold, still · Full submersion · 15 minutes</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>REPEAT</dt>
                <dd>Before every cooking session for the first 5 uses</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>WHY</dt>
                <dd>Thermal buffer prevents micro-fracture nucleation under heat</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </div>
          <div class="ritual-rite__tags">
            <span class="ritual-tag ritual-tag--clay">WATER CURE</span>
            <span class="ritual-tag ritual-tag--clay">THERMAL BUFFER</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      {%- comment -%} ── CLAY RITE 2: SLOW HEAT ── {%- endcomment -%}
      <article class="ritual-rite ritual-rite--clay" data-rite="2">
        <div class="ritual-rite__num-col">
          <span class="ritual-rite__numeral">02</span>
          <div class="ritual-rite__connector" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ritual-rite__body">
          <div class="ritual-rite__eyebrow">
            <span class="ritual-rite__devanagari">धीरे-धीरे</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__dot" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__rite-name-en">Slow Heat</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="ritual-rite__title">Thermal Patience — Never Shock the Earth</h3>
          <p class="ritual-rite__desc">
            Clay cannot absorb thermal shock. Place the vessel into a cold oven and raise temperature gradually —
            or use a heat diffuser on a gas flame. Never place a cold vessel on a high flame or into a raging oven.
            Start from ambient. Raise by 50 °C increments every 5 minutes. The clay will sing to you as it settles —
            hairline pops are normal expansion sounds, not cracking.
          </p>
          <div class="ritual-rite__specs">
            <dl class="spec-dl">
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>GAS</dt>
                <dd>Always with a heat diffuser plate · Start on minimum flame</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>OVEN</dt>
                <dd>Cold start · Max 260 °C · Raise slowly</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>FORBIDDEN</dt>
                <dd>Induction · High direct heat · Broiler element</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </div>
          <div class="ritual-rite__tags">
            <span class="ritual-tag ritual-tag--clay">GRADUAL HEAT ONLY</span>
            <span class="ritual-tag ritual-tag--clay">MAX 260 °C</span>
            <span class="ritual-tag ritual-tag--clay">DIFFUSER ON GAS</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      {%- comment -%} ── CLAY RITE 3: COOK ── {%- endcomment -%}
      <article class="ritual-rite ritual-rite--clay" data-rite="3">
        <div class="ritual-rite__num-col">
          <span class="ritual-rite__numeral">03</span>
          <div class="ritual-rite__connector" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="ritual-rite__body">
          <div class="ritual-rite__eyebrow">
            <span class="ritual-rite__devanagari">पकाना</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__dot" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
            <span class="ritual-rite__rite-name-en">Cook</span>
          </div>
          <h3 class="ritual-rite__title">Convection Braise — The Clay's Native Element</h3>
          <p class="ritual-rite__desc">
            Terre Clay vessels excel at slow thermal convection. Braises, dals, tagines, slow roasts, and
            fermentation vessels are the ideal use. The feldspathic glaze interior is non-reactive and highly
            acid-resistant — wine, tomato, and citrus braises are encouraged and will not damage the vitrified surface.
            Never attempt a dry sear or high-heat reduction inside the vessel.
          </p>
          <div class="ritual-rite__specs">
            <dl class="spec-dl">
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>IDEAL</dt>
                <dd>Braises · Dals · Tagines · Slow roasts · Ferments</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>ACIDS</dt>
                <dd>Wine · Tomato · Citrus — all safe on glazed interior</dd>
              </div>
              <div class="spec-dl__row">
                <dt>AVOID</dt>
                <dd>Dry sear · Rapid reduction · Empty vessel over heat</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </div>
          <div class="ritual-rite__tags">
